---
import Seo from '../components/Seo.astro';
import Shell from '../components/Shell.astro';
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <link rel="sitemap" href="/sitemap-index.xml" />
    <Seo
      title="How We Check — Method and Sourcing"
      description="Every figure on this site is read off the airline's own published page and stamped with the date. Here is exactly how that works, and where it falls short."
      published={new Date('2026-07-01')}
      updated={new Date('2026-07-15')}
    />
  </head>
  <body>
    <Shell section="method">
      <div class="method">
        <h1>How we check</h1>

        <p class="lede">
          The only reason to read this site instead of the airline's is that we
          tell you when we last looked. That claim is worth nothing unless the
          mechanics behind it are visible, so here they are.
        </p>

        <div class="prose">
          <h2>Where the numbers come from</h2>
          <p>
            Every fee, weight limit, and deadline is read off the carrier's own
            published page — not from a press release, not from another guide site,
            and not from memory. Each figure is stored with the date it was read and
            a link to the page it was read from. Both appear on the page you're
            looking at, next to the number itself.
          </p>
          <p>
            The figures live in one place per airline. When a fee changes, it is
            corrected once and every page quoting it updates together. This is
            deliberate: the alternative is a number written into forty articles,
            thirty-nine of which get updated.
          </p>

          <h2>What the dates mean</h2>
          <p>
            Three different dates appear on this site and they are not
            interchangeable.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Checked</strong> is when a human last compared the figure against
            the airline's page. <strong>Policy in effect since</strong> is when the
            airline's rule started applying — that's their date, not ours.
            <strong>Updated</strong> is when the article's prose last changed.
          </p>
          <p>
            An article can be months old and still correct. A figure checked
            yesterday can describe a policy that took effect two years ago. Collapsing
            these into a single "last updated" stamp is how most sites end up
            implying a freshness they don't have, and we'd rather show you three
            honest dates than one flattering one.
          </p>

          <h2>When we're overdue</h2>
          <p>
            After ninety days without a re-check, the figure flags itself on the live
            page, in red, to you. We don't wait for someone to notice internally. After
            six months, the page should come out of search results entirely rather
            than serve you a number we no longer stand behind.
          </p>
          <p>
            You will see amber and red dates on this site. That is the system
            working, not failing. A board showing only green would be a board nobody
            had reason to trust.
          </p>

          <h2>What we don't do</h2>
          <p>
            We don't bump the date on an article to make it look fresh. The
            publishing pipeline compares each article's text against its previous
            version and refuses to accept a date change with no matching content
            change — so this isn't a promise, it's a constraint.
          </p>
          <p>
            We don't generate pages from a template to cover a topic we have nothing
            to say about. Every page has to name the one question it exists to answer
            before it can be published.
          </p>
          <p>
            We don't mark up questions and answers for search engines unless they're
            visible to you on the page.
          </p>

          <h2>Where this falls short</h2>
          <p>
            Airline policy is full of exceptions that a fee table cannot hold: elite
            status, co-brand cards, route-specific rules, codeshare quirks, and agent
            discretion at the counter. We flag the ones that catch people out most
            often, but the caveats on a page are never the complete set.
          </p>
          <p>
            We are not affiliated with any airline. When a figure here disagrees with
            the airline's own page, theirs is correct and ours is stale — the link to
            check is on every card, and we'd rather you use it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Shell>

    <style>
      .method h1 {
        font-family: var(--font-display);
        font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 5vw, 2.5rem);
        font-weight: 600;
        letter-spacing: -0.025em;
        color: var(--navy);
        margin: 0 0 0.7rem;
      }
      .lede {
        font-size: 1.12rem;
        line-height: 1.6;
        max-width: 58ch;
        margin: 0 0 2.5rem;
        padding-bottom: 1.5rem;
        border-bottom: 0.5px solid var(--rule);
      }
      .method .prose { max-width: 62ch; }
    </style>
  </body>
</html>
